UNIT pin_lint
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs pinLint (its citation rule is the exported pinCited) over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse and sirLines — lint 3 of the SIR v0.5
  kernel (no pin without a citation), reproduced exactly, accidents included. Since round 5 it reads the file through the
  parser (legacy mode): the line model, directive lines, comments, quoted spans and attributes below are the parser's.
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG pin_lint(text: text) -> record[hits: list[record[line: i64, what: text, text: text]], pins: i64]
ENVELOPE text only: any length (the empty text included), any well-formed characters, every kind of line break. Non-text
  input is OUT: the real code throws a type error from its first operation.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Lint a SIR file's text for pins that name no citation. Every directive named PINNED or EARNED is a pin; a pin
  is cited when one of its citation attributes, on its own line or on a continuation line under it, has a value with
  something visible in it, or when it is an EARNED directive whose own text (its arguments or a continuation line) has
  something visible in it. The result is the list of uncited pins in line order, each as
  its line number, a fixed label and its line's text cut to 96 characters, together with the number of pins seen.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Lines. The text breaks at a carriage return followed by a line feed (one break), at a lone carriage return and at a
  lone line feed; line k counts from 1. Each line is then normalized: every U+FEFF in its leading run of SP and U+FEFF characters
  is removed (the SP characters of that run stay, in order), then its trailing SP run is removed. SP is the space U+0020 and the tab U+0009 only; every
  other whitespace (no-break space, vertical tab, form feed, U+2028, U+3000) is ordinary content. A line that is empty after
  normalization is blank. The empty text is one blank line CITED FIDELITY package
R2 Each non-blank line splits into its INDENT (its leading SP run) and its CONTENT (the rest). A line whose content begins
  with the character "#" is a COMMENT line and contributes nothing. Any other line has a CODE: its content cut at the
  comment marker (R4) when there is one, with the SP run before the marker removed; else the whole content CITED FIDELITY package
R3 Directive lines. A line without indent is a DIRECTIVE line when its NAME — its code up to the first SP or to the end —
  is NAMEFORM, or NAMEFORM followed by one colon (the colon is dropped), except that the name "CITED" (with or without the
  colon) is never a directive. Its REST is the code after the name as written, from the separating SP on. Every other
  non-blank, non-comment line is a CONTINUATION of the nearest directive line above it; with no directive line above, it is
  ignored. Blank lines and comment lines never end a directive: its continuations run to the next directive line or the
  end of the text. So an indented line, a line led by a no-break space or a vertical tab, a name glued to its text by a
  non-SP character, a lowercase word, and a line led by "CITED" are all continuations CITED FIDELITY package
R4 Quoted spans and the comment marker, per content. Scanning left to right, a quotation mark (U+0022) OPENS a span when it
  is the content's first character, or the character before it is in OPENERS, or it sits directly after the closing quotation
  mark of the previous span. The span closes at the next quotation mark, where a reverse solidus (U+005C) makes the one
  character after it ordinary (that character may itself be a quotation mark or a supplementary character). When an opened
  span finds no closing mark, it and every later quotation mark of the content open nothing. A position is INSIDE a span
  when it lies after the span's opening mark, up to and including its closing mark. The COMMENT MARKER is the first "#"
  whose previous character is SP, whose next character is SP or the end of the content, and which is not inside a span
  CITED FIDELITY package
R5 Attributes, per piece: a directive line's piece is its rest; a continuation's piece is one space followed by its code.
  An attribute is an occurrence of exactly the uppercase word "CITED", "EARNED" or "ANCHOR" in the piece with one or more
  SP characters directly before it and SP or the end of the piece directly after it, whose first letter is not inside a
  quoted span (R4, read on the piece). Its VALUE is the text from just after the word to the start of the SP run before
  the next attribute of the piece, or to the end of the piece, with leading and trailing SP removed. A directive's ARGS are
  its rest up to the SP run before its first attribute (or all of it), SP-trimmed. A continuation contributes attributes
  only, never args CITED FIDELITY package
R6 Pins. pins = the number of directive lines named PINNED or EARNED (R3: the colon form counts). A text is VISIBLE when it
  holds at least one character outside INVISIBLE. A pin is CITED iff the value of one attribute of its own line or of any of
  its continuations is VISIBLE, or it is named EARNED and its args, or the code of any one of its continuations up to the
  SP run before that piece's first attribute (all of it when it has none), is VISIBLE. A continuation's text never cites a
  PINNED. A comment, whole-line or trailing, never cites; nor does a citation word in lowercase or mixed case, glued to a
  neighbour by a non-SP character, or inside a quoted span CITED FIDELITY package
R7 hits = one entry per uncited pin in increasing line order: line = its line number (R1), what = the text "pin without a
  citation", text = its line as normalized by R1 (the trailing comment included), cut to its first 96 characters (Unicode
  scalar values: a supplementary character counts 1), or the whole of it when shorter CITED FIDELITY package
PINNED charset NAMEFORM = one uppercase ASCII letter A-Z, then any run of A-Z, 0-9 and hyphen-minus CITED the parser's directive-name form; every such name is a directive (an unknown one included), so it ends the pin above
PINNED charset OPENERS = space, tab and the 14 ASCII characters ( [ { , = : ; | < > ! & - ~ CITED the parser's quoted-span opener set (round 5b, #107); solidus, asterisk, plus sign, question mark, letters and digits are not openers
PINNED charset INVISIBLE = U+0009-U+000D, U+0020, U+0022, U+0027, U+00A0, U+00AD, U+0600-U+0605, U+061C, U+06DD, U+070F, U+0890-U+0891, U+08E2, U+1680, U+180E, U+2000-U+200F, U+2028-U+202F, U+205F-U+2064, U+2066-U+206F, U+3000, U+FEFF, U+FFF9-U+FFFB, U+110BD, U+110CD, U+13430-U+1343F, U+1BCA0-U+1BCA3, U+1D173-U+1D17A, U+E0001, U+E0020-U+E007F CITED pinCited's visible test (round 5b, #104): the JS whitespace class, General_Category Cf as of Unicode 17.0, and the two ASCII quote marks; U+0085, U+2065, curly quotes, backtick and every other character are visible
INVARIANT the number of hits never exceeds pins, and the hit lines are distinct and strictly increasing.
INVARIANT every hit text begins with PINNED or EARNED.
ORACLE value -> oracles/pin_lint.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 a6e647b4568d9b295cc816985067dfa8ab8f6315ea10a40d92a68063a9b01aba
SCOPE IN: R1-R7 over any well-formed text. OUT: non-text input; whether a citation names a real source (the lint is
  lexical); the parser's other observations (warnings, errors, the strict mode).
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (authority tree under selfhost/.authority/6199e2cc8b75), cli/sir/v05.mjs, function pinLint
  and its citation rule pinCited
  (lint 3, called by lintFamily on the primary and on every language file), over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse, sirLines,
  splitTrailingComment, splitAttributes and quotedSpans. Re-cut 2026-10-27 for REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN round 5 (FINDINGS #94); delta
  re-cut for round 5b (FINDINGS #99 #104 #107: the leading-mark run, the visible-citation rule, the narrowed openers).
